---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import { studies, levelEdges, confluenceCells, pdlCounts, BUILD_YEARS, CHECK_YEARS } from "../data/studies";
import { date, int } from "../lib/format";

const MINUS = "−";
const pts = (v: number) => (v === 0 ? "0.0" : `${v > 0 ? "+" : MINUS}${Math.abs(v).toFixed(1)}`);
const byId = Object.fromEntries(studies.map((s) => [s.id, s]));
const perYear = (n: number, years: number) => Math.round(n / years);

// Edge bars: ±5 points across the track, zero in the middle.
const SPAN = 5;
const bar = (v: number) => {
  const w = (Math.min(Math.abs(v), SPAN) / SPAN) * 50;
  return `left:${v >= 0 ? 50 : 50 - w}%;width:${w}%`;
};
const pdlShort4h = pdlCounts[0];
const pdlLong4h = pdlCounts[2];
---
<Base
  title="Studies"
  description="Measurements of how CL price behaves at swing points and fixed levels, made before a strategy is built on them. No trades, no Combine."
  crumbs={[{ label: "Studies" }]}
>
  <header class="head">
    <h1>Studies</h1>
    <p>
      A study measures how price behaves before we build a strategy on it. There are no orders, no fees and no Combine
      here, only counts and rates. Studies tell us which ideas are worth testing and which are noise.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="sum-h">
    <div class="panel-head"><h2 id="sum-h">What the studies found</h2><p>Newest first</p></div>
    <ol class="summary">
      {studies.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <time class="num faint" datetime={s.date}>{date(s.date)}</time>
          <div>
            <a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.name}</a>
            <p>{s.answer}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>The short version:</strong> a swing point or a fixed level, on its own, gives no edge. What matters is
      how price arrives and how fast it leaves. That is why the strategies built on these ideas have not passed yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="read-h">
    <div class="panel-body read">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="read-h">How to read these numbers</h2>
        <p>
          Take a swing low with a swing high above it. Price will reach one of them first. If it breaks the high first,
          the swing low <strong>held</strong>. If it breaks the low first, it <strong>failed</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Even a coin-flip market has odds here: price that sits close to the high reaches it first more often. That
          chance from distance alone is the <strong>fair rate</strong>. A study only counts what beats it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>edge</strong> is the real hold rate minus the fair rate, in percentage points. +3 means a setup
          held 3 times more in 100 than chance. Small edges are easy to find by luck when many are tested, so every
          number says how many were tried.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fair" aria-labelledby="fair-cap">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 300 200" role="img" aria-label="A swing high above and a swing low below. Price sits 30 percent of the way up, so a random market reaches the high first about 30 percent of the time.">
          <line class="lv" x1="40" y1="34" x2="270" y2="34" />
          <line class="lv" x1="40" y1="166" x2="270" y2="166" />
          <text class="lab" x="270" y="24" text-anchor="end">Swing high: break it first = held</text>
          <text class="lab" x="270" y="186" text-anchor="end">Swing low: break it first = failed</text>
          <line class="span" x1="24" y1="166" x2="24" y2="126.4" />
          <line class="rest" x1="24" y1="126.4" x2="24" y2="34" />
          <circle class="px" cx="110" cy="126.4" r="5" />
          <line class="px-line" x1="40" y1="126.4" x2="270" y2="126.4" />
          <text class="lab ink" x="122" y="118">Price now</text>
          <text class="lab sky" x="34" y="150">30%</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption id="fair-cap" class="faint">
          Price is 30% of the way from the low to the high, so the fair rate is about 30%.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id={byId["pdl-swing"].id} aria-labelledby="pdl-h">
    <div class="panel-head">
      <h2 id="pdl-h">{byId["pdl-swing"].name}</h2>
      <p>{date(byId["pdl-swing"].date)} · count only, no outcomes</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body prose">
      <p><strong>Question.</strong> {byId["pdl-swing"].question}</p>
      <p>
        <strong>The shape (short side).</strong> A 4-hour swing low forms at or below yesterday's low, after a swing
        high that was still above it. Then one of two things happens on the next two candles:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>C3:</strong> the next candle becomes the swing high, and the candle after it comes back down to the swing low.</li>
        <li><strong>C4:</strong> the candle after makes a new high first, then comes back down to the swing low.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The long side is the exact mirror, at yesterday's high. "Any swing" counts every swing point, with yesterday's
        level not used. The same count was run on 1-hour candles.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Setup</th>
            <th scope="col">Candles</th>
            <th scope="col" class="r">C3 build</th>
            <th scope="col" class="r">C4 build</th>
            <th scope="col" class="r">C3 check</th>
            <th scope="col" class="r">C4 check</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {pdlCounts.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="setup">{r.side} · {r.group}</th>
              <td class="num">{r.tf === "4h" ? "4-hour" : "1-hour"}</td>
              <td class="r num">{int(r.c3[0])}</td>
              <td class="r num">{int(r.c4[0])}</td>
              <td class="r num">{int(r.c3[1])}</td>
              <td class="r num">{int(r.c4[1])}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body prose after">
      <p>
        <strong>What it means.</strong> The 4-hour setup at yesterday's level is rare: about
        {" "}{perYear(pdlShort4h.c3[0] + pdlShort4h.c4[0], BUILD_YEARS)} short and
        {" "}{perYear(pdlLong4h.c3[0] + pdlLong4h.c4[0], BUILD_YEARS)} long a year on build data. A Combine needs more
        trades than that, so a strategy would need the 1-hour version or other setups beside it. This study does not
        say whether the shape makes money; that is the next step.
      </p>
      <p class="faint small">
        Build = Jun 2010 to Dec 2021 ({BUILD_YEARS} years), check = 2022 to 2024 ({CHECK_YEARS} years). Final-exam years
        not loaded. Two separate programs (Claude and Codex) made the same counts; spot checks on raw one-minute bars
        pass. Same-minute cases where the order is unknown are left out (10 on 1-hour, none on 4-hour).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id={byId["level-regime"].id} aria-labelledby="lr-h">
    <div class="panel-head">
      <h2 id="lr-h">{byId["level-regime"].name}</h2>
      <p>{date(byId["level-regime"].date)} · build and check years, described, not traded</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body prose">
      <p>
        <strong>Question.</strong> {byId["level-regime"].question}
        {" "}<a href="/strategies/fixed-levels/">Fixed Levels</a> trades the previous day, previous week, and 4-hour and
        daily swing points.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>How.</strong> Every first touch of each level in a day, on 5-minute candles. A bounce means price moves
        a set distance back the way it came before it moves the same distance through the level.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body edges-wrap">
      <h3 class="sub-h">A bare first touch: bounce minus fair, in points</h3>
      <div class="legend" aria-hidden="true">
        <span><i class="k-build"></i>Build 2010-2021</span>
        <span><i class="k-check"></i>Check 2022-2024</span>
      </div>
      <dl class="edges">
        {levelEdges.map((e) => (
          <div class="erow">
            <dt>{e.set}</dt>
            <dd>
              <span class="track" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="b build" style={bar(e.build)}></span>
                <span class="b check" style={bar(e.check)}></span>
              </span>
              <span class="vals num">
                <span><span class="visually-hidden">Build </span>{pts(e.build)}</span>
                <span><span class="visually-hidden">Check </span>{pts(e.check)}</span>
              </span>
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <p class="axis num faint" aria-hidden="true"><span>{MINUS}5</span><span>0</span><span>+5</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body prose after">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A touch alone is a coin flip,</strong> before and after 2021. No year is far from zero. Only the previous week's level leans to holding, by 2 to 3 points, too small to trust on its own.</li>
        <li><strong>The edge is in the fast move away.</strong> When price leaves the level in a 3-candle gap, it bounced far more often, in every year, 2022-2024 included. This partly counts the bounce itself, so it shows behaviour, not a rate you can trade.</li>
        <li><strong>The trades got worse, the market did not.</strong> Win rate 54.5% on build, 49.6% on check (353 trades). That drop is about what you expect when the best of 16 versions meets new data, plus luck.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Answer.</strong> The edge is probably smaller than build data showed, not proven gone. Only new data can
        settle it.
      </p>
      <p class="faint small">
        A first version compared bounces with a flat 50% and claimed touched levels break more than chance. A Codex
        review found the baseline was wrong; with a fair rate per touch, that claim was withdrawn.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id={byId["swing-confluence"].id} aria-labelledby="sc-h">
    <div class="panel-head">
      <h2 id="sc-h">{byId["swing-confluence"].name}</h2>
      <p>{date(byId["swing-confluence"].date)} · build years only · best of about 29,000 cells</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body prose">
      <p><strong>Question.</strong> {byId["swing-confluence"].question}</p>
      <p>
        <strong>How.</strong> {int(1914711)} swing points from 1-minute to daily candles, each marked held or failed,
        with the swing state of every timeframe from 1 minute to 1 month at that moment. Two miners (Claude and Codex)
        searched the same table separately. A pattern had to beat its fair rate by 3 points, in 9 of 12 years, and
        survive a correction for the number of patterns tried.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Baseline.</strong> A swing point alone is within 1.5 points of its fair rate on every timeframe. Price
        confirming a swing says nothing about which side breaks next.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="cells">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Pattern</th>
            <th scope="col" class="r">Edge</th>
            <th scope="col" class="r">Swings</th>
            <th scope="col">What it means</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {confluenceCells.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="pattern">{c.what}</th>
              <td class="r num">{pts(c.edge)}</td>
              <td class="r num">{int(c.n)}</td>
              <td class="means">{c.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body prose after">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The best pattern is real but too thin.</strong> Entering at the swing price earns about 2 ticks a trade after fees, roughly $3,000 a year on one contract. With a limit order a little lower, as our fill rules require, it earns nothing: the limit fills mostly on the swings that fail.</li>
        <li><strong>The owner's three theories did not pass.</strong> A swing that breaks the small timeframe but not the big one, a higher-timeframe candle still forming, and the first take of yesterday's low or high: 148 cells, none passed. Yesterday's level leaned +2.4 to +2.7 points.</li>
        <li><strong>How price arrives matters more than which timeframes agree.</strong> Slow or fast, deep or shallow in the bigger range: that is where the small edges were.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="faint small">
        A Codex audit of the first table found 8 defects; all were fixed, the table rebuilt and every scan re-run. The
        early 1-minute "findings" vanished: they came from tick rounding. Check and final-exam years were not touched.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="disc faint">
    <Icon name="warn" size={13} /> Every number here is in-sample: measured on years we have already seen. A study shows
    behaviour on past data. It is not a trading signal and not financial advice.
  </p>
</Base>
<style>
  .head { position: relative; padding: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.25rem) 0 1.75rem; }
  .head::before {
    content: ""; position: absolute; z-index: -1; inset: -3.5rem -20% auto; height: 22rem; pointer-events: none;
    background: radial-gradient(34rem 16rem at 25% 0%, var(--sky-glow), transparent 70%);
  }
  .head p { margin-top: 0.8rem; max-width: 64ch; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .panel + .panel { margin-top: 1rem; }
  .panel { scroll-margin-top: 4.5rem; }

  .summary { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0.4rem 1.25rem 0; }
  .summary li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 7.5rem 1fr; gap: 1rem; padding: 0.85rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .summary li:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .summary time { font-size: 0.8rem; padding-top: 0.15rem; }
  .summary a { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; text-decoration-color: var(--line-hi); }
  .summary a:hover { text-decoration-color: currentColor; }
  .summary p { margin-top: 0.2rem; color: var(--ink-2); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .note { padding: 0.9rem 1.25rem 1.1rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 90ch; }
  .note strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }

  .read { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 20rem; gap: 2rem; align-items: center; }
  .read h2 { color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
  .fair { margin: 0; }
  .fair svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .fair figcaption { margin-top: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.8rem; }
  .lv { stroke: var(--line-hi); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .px-line { stroke: var(--ink-3); stroke-width: 1; stroke-dasharray: 3 4; }
  .px { fill: var(--ink); }
  .span { stroke: var(--sky); stroke-width: 3; stroke-linecap: round; }
  .rest { stroke: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.14); stroke-width: 3; stroke-linecap: round; }
  .lab { fill: var(--ink-3); font: 500 11px var(--sans); }
  .lab.ink { fill: var(--ink); }
  .lab.sky { fill: var(--sky-ink); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }

  .prose p strong:first-child { color: var(--ink); }
  .after { max-width: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .after > * { max-width: 68ch; }
  .small { font-size: 0.84rem; }
  .prose a { color: var(--sky-ink); }

  th.setup, th.pattern { color: var(--ink); font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 500; }
  .table-wrap { padding: 0 0.5rem; }
  .cells th.pattern { white-space: normal; min-width: 16rem; max-width: 26rem; vertical-align: top; }
  .cells td { vertical-align: top; }
  .cells td.means { white-space: normal; color: var(--ink-2); min-width: 16rem; }

  .edges-wrap { padding-top: 0; }
  .sub-h { font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--ink); }
  .legend { display: flex; gap: 1rem; margin: 0.6rem 0 0.9rem; font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .legend span { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem; }
  .legend i { width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 2px; }
  .k-build, .b.build { background: rgb(236 239 245 / 0.42); }
  .k-check, .b.check { background: var(--sky); }
  .edges { margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 0.55rem; }
  .erow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 8.5rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1rem; align-items: center; }
  .erow dt { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .erow dd { margin: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 6.5rem; gap: 1rem; align-items: center; }
  .track {
    position: relative; display: grid; gap: 3px; height: 1.6rem; align-content: center;
    background: linear-gradient(var(--line-hi), var(--line-hi)) 50% 0 / 1px 100% no-repeat;
  }
  .track::before { content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 0; border-radius: 3px; background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.025); }
  .b { position: relative; display: block; height: 7px; border-radius: 2px; }
  .vals { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--ink); }
  .vals span:last-child { color: var(--sky-ink); }
  .axis { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin: 0.4rem 0 0 9.5rem; width: calc(100% - 9.5rem - 7.5rem); font-size: 0.7rem; }

  .disc { display: flex; gap: 0.45rem; align-items: baseline; margin: 1.25rem 0 0; font-size: 0.8rem; max-width: 90ch; }
  .disc :global(svg) { flex: none; color: var(--warn); transform: translateY(2px); }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .read { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.25rem; }
    .fair { max-width: 22rem; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .summary li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.25rem; }
    .erow { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.35rem; }
    .erow dd { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 5.5rem; gap: 0.75rem; }
    .axis { margin-left: 0; width: calc(100% - 5.5rem - 0.75rem); }
  }
</style>
